{
  "dom_id": "step-q43827",
  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "Consider a vector space $V$ and vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\in V.$ Given that ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{4}}$ is a linearly independent set and ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},\\text{\u00a0}{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is a linearly dependent one, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${v}_{3}$ is a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$, ${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{4}$\n- ${v}_{4}$ is a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$\n- ${v}_{2}$ is a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{4}$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's examine each of the given options in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is a linearly dependent set, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}+{x}_{4}{v}_{4}=0\n$$\n\nhas a non-zero solution. Moreover, ${x}_{3}$ can't be zero because then\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{4}{v}_{4}=0\n$$\n\nwill have a non-zero solution and that's a contradiction as ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{4}}$ is linearly independent.\nTherefore, from ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}+{x}_{4}{v}_{4}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{v}_{3}=-\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{3}}{v}_{1}-\\frac{{x}_{2}}{{x}_{3}}{v}_{2}-\\frac{{x}_{4}}{{x}_{3}}{v}_{4}.\n$$\n\nThis means that ${v}_{3}$ is a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$, ${v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{4}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{4}}$ is a linearly independent set, ${v}_{4}$ can not be a linear combination of other vectors.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{4}}$ is a linearly independent set, ${v}_{2}$ can not be a linear combination of other vectors.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
